winefile: paint list rows in the system colours.

Vessel themes the guest dark through HKCU\Control Panel\Colors, which Wine
honours everywhere except here. winefile owner-draws its two panes and paints
each row with literals: white behind every row, black text on it, and navy
behind the selected one. So a dark desktop came up with two bright white strips
across the middle of the only window in it, and no registry value could reach
them -- they are compiled in.

The values it wants already exist as system colours, and the mapping is exact:
the row background is COLOR_WINDOW, its text COLOR_WINDOWTEXT, and the focused
row is the COLOR_HIGHLIGHT / COLOR_HIGHLIGHTTEXT pair that every other Win32
list uses. Reading them through GetSysColor also fixes the reverse case, which
the literals got wrong on stock Wine too: a user running a light desktop with a
dark high-contrast scheme, or any scheme at all, was shown white regardless.

COLOR_COMPRESSED is deliberately left alone. It is winefile's own semantic
marker for a compressed file rather than a piece of chrome, it has no system
colour to map onto, and blue on the Vessel background is legible.

Not upstreamed. It is a small, uncontroversial change and worth sending, but
this tree is pinned to wine-11.14 and carries it as a patch either way.

diff --git a/programs/winefile/winefile.c b/programs/winefile/winefile.c
index e37f3371bab..515bc0938c7 100644
--- a/programs/winefile/winefile.c
+++ b/programs/winefile/winefile.c
@@ -2674,13 +2674,13 @@ static void draw_item(Pane* pane, LPDRAWITEMSTRUCT dis, Entry* entry, int calcWi
 		if (attrs & FILE_ATTRIBUTE_COMPRESSED)
 			textcolor = COLOR_COMPRESSED;
 		else
-			textcolor = RGB(0,0,0);
+			textcolor = GetSysColor(COLOR_WINDOWTEXT);

 		if (dis->itemState & ODS_FOCUS) {
-			textcolor = RGB(255,255,255);
-			bkcolor = COLOR_SELECTION;
+			textcolor = GetSysColor(COLOR_HIGHLIGHTTEXT);
+			bkcolor = GetSysColor(COLOR_HIGHLIGHT);
 		} else {
-			bkcolor = RGB(255,255,255);
+			bkcolor = GetSysColor(COLOR_WINDOW);
 		}

 		hbrush = CreateSolidBrush(bkcolor);
